  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Patterns of Cairo / Megawra</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Patterns of Cairo&#x2019;s objective is to document, digitise and democratise knowledge on the design elements found in the heritage buildings of Historic Cairo. This is is achieved through creating an online curated platform that allows scholars, designers and the general public to access representative visual elements which are aesthetically and historically accurate with comprehensive data on provenance, style, development, and so forth. The project aims to contribute to creative economy sector in Egypt by providing easy access to the rich patterns and history of the Historic City for artists and designers while simultaneously offering them the same platform to promote their work and get inspired from others inspiring further collaborations. The patterns will be available in vector format to enable easy incorporation in the design process. They will be linked to images that convey colour, material, texture, scale, and context, with cross-referencing tools that facilitate comparison and triangulation. Patterns of Cairo will be the first design-oriented open-source platform with thoroughly curated and catered resources on the patterns of Historic Cairo and the buildings they adorn. In addition to the preservation component which the project will digitally provide, the online database will raise awareness and promote ownership of Historic Cairo&#x2019;s design. The platform will connect a network of designers and makers from the MENA region and beyond to harness the richness of designs from this era as a resource for contemporary application in the creative economy. Users will also have the opportunity to promote their work and the designs they produce using this resource on the platform. To facilitate a tailored user-friendly platform, disseminate it, and enable designers to explore its potential, two workshops will be organised in collaboration between Egyptian and Dutch designers; the first to brainstorm the platform&#x2019;s structure and content, and the second to experiment with the website and explore its potential as a design tool.</narrative>
  </description>
